{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "NSE Bhavcopy Join — Portfolio Prices with VLOOKUP, INDEX-MATCH & XLOOKUP",
  "scenario": "You manage a Mumbai equity desk portfolio tracker. NSE's bhavcopy (market data export) arrives daily with symbol, open, high, low, close, volume. Your Portfolio sheet holds tickers and quantities. Build three lookup columns — one using VLOOKUP, one using INDEX-MATCH, one using XLOOKUP — to pull today's closing prices, then compute portfolio value. Compare results and understand why INDEX-MATCH and XLOOKUP are now desk standards.",
  "instructions": "Build three parallel lookup columns (F, G, H) to fetch NSE closing prices into your portfolio. Use VLOOKUP in column F, INDEX-MATCH in column G, and XLOOKUP in column H. Then compute portfolio value (quantity × price) and answer analysis questions on data integrity and cross-lookup validation.",
  "learning_objective": "Execute VLOOKUP, INDEX-MATCH, and XLOOKUP to join two tables by ticker; identify lookup flaws and fixes; validate results; compute portfolio value using multi-method lookups.",
  "hints": [
    "VLOOKUP scans the first column of its table and counts columns from left. INDEX-MATCH scans any column you name and returns from any column independently. XLOOKUP does both in one call and defaults to exact match.",
    "For VLOOKUP, pin the bhavcopy table ($A$2:$F$11) so it doesn't drift when you copy down. For INDEX-MATCH, MATCH finds the row number in the ticker column, then INDEX fetches from the close column. For XLOOKUP, specify exact match mode (0) and a polite 'Not traded' if missing.",
    "VLOOKUP formula: =VLOOKUP(B2, $A$2:$F$11, 5, 0). INDEX-MATCH: =INDEX($F$2:$F$11, MATCH(B2, $A$2:$A$11, 0)). XLOOKUP (if available): =XLOOKUP(B2, $A$2:$A$11, $F$2:$F$11, \"Not traded\", 0). All three should return the same closing price for each ticker."
  ],
